1
00:00:00,730 --> 00:00:04,900
<i>A skeleton dating 200 years back
found in the Himalayas,</i>

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:08,400
<i>matched with the DNA
of his missing step-sister.</i>

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,190
Shiharu?

4
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:13,250
It's true.

5
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:14,350
I believe you.

6
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:16,160
Haruka, there's something
you still don't know.

7
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,270
I wish to ask about my uncle's death.

8
00:00:19,270 --> 00:00:22,710
Mari is gone.

9
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:24,580
Ishimizaki's daughter?

10
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:28,050
Why don't we work together,
for the sake of our own goals?

11
00:00:31,790 --> 00:00:34,550
The remains Haruka was analyzing...

12
00:00:34,550 --> 00:00:38,500
Were they given to Ishimizaki by Amol?

13
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:39,950
Yes, sir.

14
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:42,790
<i>But now, this Amol has gone missing.</i>

15
00:00:43,790 --> 00:00:48,900
He was to attend a rather important meeting,

16
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,420
<i>but continues to remain out of contact.</i>

17
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:55,070
Police are currently searching
for his whereabouts.

18
00:00:55,070 --> 00:00:58,670
Is this possibly related
to Professor Ishimizaki's case?

19
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:02,550
We have to erase all evidence of "log.0".

20
00:01:02,550 --> 00:01:05,700
I will reassess our documents
on our associates.

21
00:01:07,130 --> 00:01:08,720
If you'll excuse me.

22
00:01:17,270 --> 00:01:22,890
The disappearance of those bones
and my uncle's death is no coincidence.

23
00:01:22,890 --> 00:01:24,080
I agree.

24
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:29,150
Maybe a relative or loved one
wanted the remains back?

25
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:31,290
Maybe.

26
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:34,420
Was there anything odd about these bones?

27
00:01:36,210 --> 00:01:38,460
You know something.

28
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:40,420
Please, tell me everything.

29
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:49,350
You left my uncle a voicemail.

30
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:54,290
It sounded urgent.

31
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:56,090
What were you going to ask about?

32
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:58,080
Stuff.

33
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:06,290
Do you know what a postdoc is?

34
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:09,380
They're essentially temp researchers.

35
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:11,370
Almost like a dispatch worker?

36
00:02:11,370 --> 00:02:16,280
You could put it like that.

37
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:21,530
Postdocs either continue their research,
or give up and find jobs elsewhere.

38
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:24,810
Not the sort of conversation
I'd like to have in his lab.

39
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:27,190
You're lying.

40
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:30,370
My uncle often spoke of you.

41
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:33,980
He said you never consulted in anyone.

42
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:37,040
He hoped you one day would.

43
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:46,090
I'm just worried about Mari.

44
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:51,730
The culprit murdered my uncle.

45
00:02:51,730 --> 00:02:54,210
They stole human remains without remorse.

46
00:02:54,210 --> 00:02:57,430
What could they be doing to that poor girl?

47
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:08,970
We may find something
in the Professor's office.

48
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:15,560
This is where the bones were stolen.

49
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:18,900
But the lab is almost never empty.

50
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:21,440
There's a camera right outside as well.

51
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:24,480
And yet, the security tapes were wiped.

52
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:28,300
The culprit could enter here
without suspicion,

53
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:32,290
and knew where the cameras were...

54
00:03:32,290 --> 00:03:35,620
Hey, I was in the middle of an interrogation.

55
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:37,250
I know. I don't doubt you.

56
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:41,380
The matching DNA sample
was also stolen, right?

57
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:42,790
Yeah.

58
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:48,810
What?

59
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:09,640
Professor Ishimizaki often holed up here
when he needed time alone.

60
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:27,430
Is this Mari?

61
00:04:32,230 --> 00:04:34,960
I see she used to be so full of energy.

62
00:04:36,170 --> 00:04:37,710
She was.

63
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:43,290
Did you know of her circumstances?

64
00:04:43,290 --> 00:04:46,830
Yeah. I met her once.

65
00:04:46,830 --> 00:04:48,360
When?

66
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:53,260
<i>One of the times I'd gone to Yamashiro Park.</i>

67
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:55,650
<i>I only stopped to say hello.</i>

68
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:57,450
Is this your daughter?

69
00:04:59,790 --> 00:05:03,440
Yes. Her name is Mari.

70
00:05:05,730 --> 00:05:07,440
Hello there.

71
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:14,250
[ishimizaki]

72
00:05:14,250 --> 00:05:16,610
Do you know his password?

73
00:05:16,610 --> 00:05:20,330
There's the one all our researchers use.

74
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:25,830
Guess that's not it.

75
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:28,700
Mind if I try?

76
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:30,920
[mari0808]

77
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:35,770
Mari's birthday.

78
00:05:35,770 --> 00:05:38,020
Makes sense.

79
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:43,710
[Inbox - Search: Roopkund]

80
00:05:44,350 --> 00:05:47,140
[No matching results]

81
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:51,510
Let's check his browsing history.

82
00:05:55,650 --> 00:05:56,890
[Kayoko Senba]

83
00:05:56,890 --> 00:05:59,070
Who is that?

84
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:01,160
Kayoko Senba?

85
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:04,370
<i>She's a world-renowned
developmental biologist.</i>

86
00:06:04,370 --> 00:06:08,430
<i>Now that I think about it,
I saw her at Professor Ishimizaki's funeral.</i>

87
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:11,530
[Kayoko Senba, Guest Lecture]

88
00:06:11,530 --> 00:06:14,930
Maybe he was going to attend her lecture?

89
00:06:21,740 --> 00:06:22,870
[10:00AM-11:00AM - S]

90
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:25,500
That's the day he was murdered.

91
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:27,840
"S"...

92
00:06:29,010 --> 00:06:31,090
"Senba"?

93
00:06:31,090 --> 00:06:34,720
Did he have plans to see her that day?

94
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:55,620
What?

95
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:01,320
I heard people inside, but...

96
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:04,080
What are you doing here?

97
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:10,980
I accidentally deleted the thesis
I sent Professor Ishimizaki.

98
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:13,660
I hoped he had the email backed up.

99
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:16,230
I see.

100
00:07:17,610 --> 00:07:19,670
I am Ishimizaki's niece.

101
00:07:19,670 --> 00:07:21,800
It's nice to meet you.

102
00:07:23,910 --> 00:07:26,000
Looks like he doesn't have it.

103
00:07:26,690 --> 00:07:29,260
Guess I'll check my files again.

104
00:07:29,260 --> 00:07:30,910
Sure.

105
00:07:30,910 --> 00:07:33,260
- Shall we?
- Right.

106
00:07:39,090 --> 00:07:41,070
I'll be locking up.

107
00:07:41,070 --> 00:07:42,680
Okay.

108
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:46,680
Sorry, I let my guard down.

109
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:50,840
So, my uncle knew this Kayoko Senba.

110
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:52,490
Yeah.

111
00:07:52,490 --> 00:07:54,410
Should we ask her about it?

112
00:07:54,410 --> 00:07:55,970
Ask who?

113
00:07:56,710 --> 00:07:58,180
You know.

114
00:08:12,940 --> 00:08:14,710
[A mystery spanning two centuries]
[Matching the DNA of his step-sister]

115
00:08:14,710 --> 00:08:16,790
Sir, your guest.

116
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:26,490
[log.2]

117
00:08:26,490 --> 00:08:28,830
A diner would have sufficed.

118
00:08:28,830 --> 00:08:31,160
Our conversation requires privacy, no?

119
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:32,330
I suppose.

120
00:08:32,330 --> 00:08:35,920
- Want a drink?
- Water is enough, thank you.

121
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:42,960
Are you eating well?

122
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:46,440
For the most part.

123
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:48,790
Except when I'm busy.

124
00:08:49,990 --> 00:08:52,400
I used to be just like you.

125
00:08:53,410 --> 00:08:56,070
And Professor Ishimizaki as well?

126
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:01,840
I remember in our early days as researchers,

127
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:07,080
we'd go to horrendously cheap bars,

128
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:09,980
and talk bad about all our professors.

129
00:09:16,190 --> 00:09:18,960
His passing deeply saddens me.

130
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:26,960
And while I'd rather not badmouth the dead,

131
00:09:27,670 --> 00:09:31,170
those remains he had you analyze...

132
00:09:31,170 --> 00:09:36,040
I suspect he acquired them through...
less scrupulous means.

133
00:09:37,780 --> 00:09:40,030
What?

134
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:44,120
<i>I hear over 800 human remains
were discovered around Roopkund.</i>

135
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:47,960
<i>All of which are precious for research.</i>

136
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:52,490
<i>So why analyze the DNA
of one particular skeleton?</i>

137
00:09:53,160 --> 00:09:55,960
I assume it was the work assigned to him.

138
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:59,950
This group of remains
spans generations, and nationalities.

139
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:02,930
Why analyze just one?

140
00:10:05,330 --> 00:10:08,250
I suppose it is a bit odd.

141
00:10:08,250 --> 00:10:10,580
What are you getting at?

142
00:10:10,580 --> 00:10:17,080
There are collectors out there
who gather excavated human remains.

143
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:20,430
But those skeletons must first be analyzed
before they're sold.

144
00:10:21,530 --> 00:10:23,670
Professor Ishimizaki
would never entertain the idea.

145
00:10:23,670 --> 00:10:25,850
I agree with you.

146
00:10:27,340 --> 00:10:29,020
However...

147
00:10:31,110 --> 00:10:36,290
The researcher who sent him the bones
has gone missing in India.

148
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:47,580
Ishimizaki may have been involved
in some trouble.

149
00:10:49,730 --> 00:10:54,500
He may have roped you into it as well.

150
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:03,030
You mentioned the DNA of the remains
was a perfect match to Shiharu's.

151
00:11:03,650 --> 00:11:06,410
Yes. I redid the analysis multiple times.

152
00:11:06,410 --> 00:11:10,210
I don't doubt you. I know you're thorough.

153
00:11:10,210 --> 00:11:16,560
But maybe the error
was in the DNA sample itself.

154
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:19,650
Impossible. I know that was
Shiharu's hair I used.

155
00:11:19,650 --> 00:11:24,750
<i>You had Ishimizaki register it
during orientation, no?</i>

156
00:11:24,750 --> 00:11:27,740
<i>You were still inexperienced at the time.</i>

157
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:31,410
<i>It's entirely possible he tricked you.</i>
[Shiharu Nanase]

158
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:35,790
Why? What would his motive be?

159
00:11:35,790 --> 00:11:38,710
I can't imagine his reasoning.

160
00:11:38,710 --> 00:11:41,330
But it's my only working theory.

161
00:11:41,330 --> 00:11:47,410
Shiharu's DNA couldn't match
that of a skeleton from two centuries ago.

162
00:11:47,410 --> 00:11:49,150
Ultimately...

163
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:56,960
I'm not sure I trust Ishimizaki anymore.

164
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:07,190
Will you promise to keep this discussion
between us?

165
00:12:09,410 --> 00:12:12,830
What we've said here
could tarnish his reputation.

166
00:12:12,830 --> 00:12:16,190
It could put you in danger as well.

167
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:19,970
Promise me you won't do anything rash.

168
00:12:24,730 --> 00:12:30,560
I can't stand to see you hurt any more.

169
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:33,210
Go see Dr. Furukawa.

170
00:12:33,210 --> 00:12:35,880
She is an excellent psychiatrist.

171
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:38,350
I know she will be of use to you.

172
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:43,050
Alright.

173
00:12:43,050 --> 00:12:44,800
I promise to go see her.

174
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:46,290
Really?

175
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:53,760
Professor Ishimizaki also said
I shouldn't worry you any further.

176
00:12:55,730 --> 00:12:57,470
I see.

177
00:13:04,910 --> 00:13:09,630
Isn't this the restaurant where we first met?

178
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:13,180
It took you this long to realize?

179
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:16,370
That's right.

180
00:13:16,980 --> 00:13:19,670
This is indeed where we first met.

181
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:28,840
Wow, I've never been somewhere so fancy.

182
00:13:28,840 --> 00:13:31,560
- Do you dine here often?
- No, not often.

183
00:13:33,070 --> 00:13:37,630
Haruka, this man will become your father.

184
00:13:39,300 --> 00:13:43,050
My name is Kyoichi Nanase.
It's nice to finally meet you.

185
00:13:44,910 --> 00:13:49,370
Kyoichi also has a daughter named Shiharu.

186
00:13:49,370 --> 00:13:51,960
I'm sure you two will learn to get along.

187
00:13:51,960 --> 00:13:55,900
I'm sorry she couldn't join us today.
She was feeling sick.

188
00:13:56,790 --> 00:13:59,410
No need to act so formal.

189
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:02,690
Want something to drink?

190
00:14:31,650 --> 00:14:33,350
You're early.

191
00:14:33,350 --> 00:14:36,210
The moving truck will be here in a moment.

192
00:14:36,210 --> 00:14:39,110
Shiharu! Your mother and brother
have arrived.

193
00:14:39,110 --> 00:14:40,890
Come on in.

194
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:54,980
Haruka, don't be a stranger. Come in.

195
00:15:01,870 --> 00:15:07,680
I want you to remember me
with those eyes of yours.

196
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:11,720
Always.

197
00:15:30,250 --> 00:15:32,360
Mother, I'll put your smoothie here.

198
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:33,590
Thank you.

199
00:15:33,590 --> 00:15:35,600
Ubongo!

200
00:15:36,200 --> 00:15:39,630
Wow, you got one already!

201
00:15:39,630 --> 00:15:42,710
- A blue?
- Yes, one blue.

202
00:15:42,710 --> 00:15:44,910
What color will be next?

203
00:15:46,940 --> 00:15:48,670
You peeked!

204
00:15:48,670 --> 00:15:50,870
Nuh-uh!

205
00:15:50,870 --> 00:15:53,930
- See?
- Okay, okay.

206
00:16:01,490 --> 00:16:02,960
Something the matter?

207
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:04,790
No, nothing.

208
00:16:04,790 --> 00:16:09,300
That girl is one smart cookie, I tell you.

209
00:16:10,250 --> 00:16:12,480
Grandma, again!

210
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:14,100
Yes, of course.

211
00:16:15,170 --> 00:16:18,100
You won't win a second time.

212
00:16:18,910 --> 00:16:20,520
I'll be heading out.

213
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:22,770
Mom's heading out later?

214
00:16:22,770 --> 00:16:26,370
- Yeah. She's lecturing at Meiei Uni today.
- I see.

215
00:16:26,370 --> 00:16:27,600
Good luck, Mom.

216
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:30,790
- Thanks.
- Bye, Daddy!

217
00:16:32,920 --> 00:16:37,110
- Seven!
- Alright, seven. How do I play it?

218
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:10,220
I hear Haruka Nanase was a real mess
after his sister went missing?

219
00:17:10,220 --> 00:17:11,960
Seems like it.

220
00:17:17,740 --> 00:17:22,770
He self isolated, never spoke to anyone,

221
00:17:22,770 --> 00:17:26,660
and always muttered to himself at night.

222
00:17:26,660 --> 00:17:27,980
Yikes.

223
00:17:27,980 --> 00:17:29,400
Agreed.

224
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:32,430
Sounds like a precarious individual,
doesn't he?

225
00:17:34,190 --> 00:17:39,020
I thought you promised your father
you'd stay out of the case?

226
00:17:39,020 --> 00:17:41,730
I only said that so he wouldn't worry.

227
00:17:41,730 --> 00:17:45,230
- But I will go to the clinic.
- Are you ill?

228
00:17:45,230 --> 00:17:48,490
My father assumes I'm acting delusional.

229
00:17:48,490 --> 00:17:50,440
I see...

230
00:17:50,440 --> 00:17:52,630
I'm not, alright?

231
00:17:52,630 --> 00:17:56,110
So, what if Kayoko Senba ignores us?

232
00:17:56,110 --> 00:17:59,160
You should smile.

233
00:17:59,160 --> 00:18:00,480
Huh?

234
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:04,570
I said, you should smile.

235
00:18:04,570 --> 00:18:06,350
I have nothing to smile about.

236
00:18:06,350 --> 00:18:09,230
How can you, when Mari is missing?

237
00:18:09,230 --> 00:18:12,800
"We don't laugh because we're happy -
we're happy, because we laugh."

238
00:18:13,330 --> 00:18:14,670
What?

239
00:18:14,670 --> 00:18:17,620
It's a quote from the philosopher,
William James.

240
00:18:17,620 --> 00:18:22,330
I want to smile,
no matter how depressed I am.

241
00:18:22,330 --> 00:18:25,350
I don't want to waste my life
wallowing in despair.

242
00:18:25,350 --> 00:18:27,960
That makes no sense.

243
00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:29,690
You should give it a try.

244
00:18:29,690 --> 00:18:32,890
Come on, smile!

245
00:18:35,480 --> 00:18:37,090
Satisfied?

246
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:40,560
It'll do.

247
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:43,580
[Kayoko Senba]

248
00:18:43,580 --> 00:18:49,210
Humanity has slowly unraveled
the mysteries of life

249
00:18:49,210 --> 00:18:55,730
by artificially manipulating,
and mimicking life's many phenomena.

250
00:18:55,730 --> 00:19:02,150
Yet new discoveries in biology
are always met with criticism

251
00:19:02,150 --> 00:19:07,610
from ethical,
and religious standpoints alike.

252
00:19:08,250 --> 00:19:12,640
Both parties say we are
"bastardizing the miracle of life".

253
00:19:13,310 --> 00:19:19,590
Yet know that we, biologists,

254
00:19:19,590 --> 00:19:26,640
work with an utter respect for life.

255
00:19:30,370 --> 00:19:33,580
This young boy is Keita.

256
00:19:33,580 --> 00:19:37,340
He is my beloved grandchild.

257
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:45,580
Keita was born with a rare condition
called, "Phenylketonuria".

258
00:19:46,090 --> 00:19:47,990
Those with phenylketonuria

259
00:19:47,990 --> 00:19:52,960
are unable to process the amino acid,
phenylalanine.

260
00:19:52,960 --> 00:19:57,890
As a result, they suffer from...

261
00:20:08,690 --> 00:20:10,310
What is it?

262
00:20:10,310 --> 00:20:12,480
- Stay here.
- Huh?

263
00:20:35,850 --> 00:20:40,500
To those here who are involved in biology.

264
00:20:40,500 --> 00:20:44,440
I hope you take great pride in your work,

265
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:49,560
and continue your research, without fail.

266
00:20:52,220 --> 00:20:56,640
Thank you very much for listening.

267
00:21:26,580 --> 00:21:28,160
Where did you go?

268
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:30,250
I'll explain later. Where's Kayoko Senba?

269
00:21:30,250 --> 00:21:31,880
She's probably on her way out.

270
00:21:31,880 --> 00:21:34,200
There's an exit out back.

271
00:21:41,950 --> 00:21:44,400
Thank you, Dr. Senba.

272
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:46,490
Right this way.

273
00:21:46,490 --> 00:21:49,440
- Excuse us.
- Did you need something?

274
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:51,110
We would like to speak with Dr. Senba.

275
00:21:51,110 --> 00:21:52,750
I will take your questions.

276
00:21:52,750 --> 00:21:55,330
- If you'll give us your contact...
- We'd like to speak to her directly.

277
00:21:55,330 --> 00:21:57,780
Dr. Senba isn't accepting any assistants.

278
00:21:57,780 --> 00:22:02,470
- No, that's not...
- Jeez, Haruka. Don't be shy.

279
00:22:08,140 --> 00:22:12,720
I had just hoped to get her autograph.

280
00:22:14,370 --> 00:22:17,240
Please don't take too much of her time.

281
00:22:17,240 --> 00:22:18,880
Of course.

282
00:22:24,920 --> 00:22:26,710
Smile.

283
00:22:29,040 --> 00:22:30,830
Dr. Senba.

284
00:22:30,830 --> 00:22:32,200
Yes?

285
00:22:33,560 --> 00:22:34,920
I'm a huge fan.

286
00:22:34,920 --> 00:22:39,300
May I have your autograph?

287
00:22:40,250 --> 00:22:41,730
Oh my.

288
00:22:42,320 --> 00:22:45,330
To think I had such young fans.

289
00:22:45,330 --> 00:22:48,000
- Shall I hold that?
- Thank you.

290
00:22:48,570 --> 00:22:50,460
Will a simple message do?

291
00:22:50,460 --> 00:22:51,570
Of course.

292
00:22:51,570 --> 00:22:53,840
What is your name?

293
00:22:54,850 --> 00:22:56,790
Nanase.

294
00:22:58,620 --> 00:23:00,550
"Nanase".

295
00:23:04,900 --> 00:23:09,500
What does your father do?

296
00:23:09,500 --> 00:23:11,830
He operates Nichie Pharmaceutical.

297
00:23:11,830 --> 00:23:14,990
Ah, Kyoichi Nanase.

298
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:22,500
You knew of Professor Ishimizaki, yes?

299
00:23:22,500 --> 00:23:25,160
I did. What about him?

300
00:23:25,770 --> 00:23:28,730
I currently work for his laboratory.

301
00:23:32,900 --> 00:23:37,200
How do you know my father,
and Professor Ishimizaki?

302
00:23:39,080 --> 00:23:41,020
We used to do work together.

303
00:23:41,020 --> 00:23:42,350
What sort of work?

304
00:23:42,350 --> 00:23:44,660
I cannot say I remember.

305
00:23:44,660 --> 00:23:46,590
It's been so long.

306
00:23:46,590 --> 00:23:49,620
Doctor, you attended the Professor's funeral.

307
00:23:49,620 --> 00:23:50,810
Dr. Senba must be going.

308
00:23:50,810 --> 00:23:55,460
Please, don't you know anything
about Roopkund?

309
00:23:56,030 --> 00:23:58,200
What are you talking about?

310
00:23:58,200 --> 00:24:01,100
It's fine, he's no bother.

311
00:24:01,100 --> 00:24:04,680
- But...
- You may leave us.

312
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:05,770
If you insist.

313
00:24:05,770 --> 00:24:07,420
Thank you again, Doctor.

314
00:24:12,230 --> 00:24:13,290
Those remains...

315
00:24:13,290 --> 00:24:15,040
Not another word.

316
00:24:17,320 --> 00:24:19,790
Do not look into it any further.

317
00:24:19,790 --> 00:24:22,260
I say this for your own sake.

318
00:24:24,370 --> 00:24:26,140
Now be on your way.

319
00:24:39,480 --> 00:24:41,160
What did she mean by that?

320
00:24:41,800 --> 00:24:43,350
I don't know.

321
00:24:43,350 --> 00:24:48,720
But at the very least,
we've acquired Kayoko Senba's DNA.

322
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:02,130
Come in.

323
00:25:03,480 --> 00:25:05,240
Sir.

324
00:25:15,290 --> 00:25:19,970
All documents which cover "log.0"
have been disposed of.

325
00:25:21,400 --> 00:25:25,490
Do you recall the man we encountered,
named Yoichi Onodera?

326
00:25:25,490 --> 00:25:28,990
<i>He's a freelance writer, who makes a killing
by blackmailing celebrities.</i>

327
00:25:28,990 --> 00:25:30,950
I remember him.

328
00:25:30,950 --> 00:25:33,470
He seems to be looking into "log.0".

329
00:25:36,660 --> 00:25:40,760
If he finds a lead,
he may sell the intel to Xin Ming Ah.

330
00:25:43,480 --> 00:25:45,630
I understand.

331
00:25:45,630 --> 00:25:47,350
Give me some time to think.

332
00:25:54,440 --> 00:25:57,810
You must be Mr. Kojima,
the Japanese rep for Xin Ming Ah.

333
00:25:59,370 --> 00:26:03,440
I hear Xin Ming Ah wants to buy out
Nichie Pharmaceutical.

334
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:05,860
But why Nichie Pharmaceutical?

335
00:26:05,860 --> 00:26:09,940
Because you thought they'd be a cheap buy?

336
00:26:09,940 --> 00:26:13,450
Their founder, Yumihiko Nanase,
is surrounded by nasty rumors.

337
00:26:13,450 --> 00:26:16,160
Did you catch onto that?

338
00:26:16,160 --> 00:26:18,750
- Did you?
- But of course.

339
00:26:18,750 --> 00:26:24,350
Perhaps we even made the same realization.

340
00:26:25,550 --> 00:26:27,500
And that is?

341
00:26:30,880 --> 00:26:33,230
I'm referring to the Plantae.

342
00:26:38,230 --> 00:26:41,850
If you want my intel, call me here.

343
00:26:41,850 --> 00:26:47,000
Secure their sore point,
and the buy out will be a cinch.

344
00:26:56,460 --> 00:26:58,920
I'm picking one hell of a fight here!

345
00:26:58,920 --> 00:27:01,020
A guy with no sponsor,

346
00:27:01,020 --> 00:27:04,500
raring up against a mega corp.

347
00:27:04,500 --> 00:27:08,400
That guy will call back any minute now.

348
00:27:08,400 --> 00:27:11,450
I sure got a big fish this time!

349
00:27:11,450 --> 00:27:15,510
- The hell?
- This is my chance at redemption.

350
00:27:15,510 --> 00:27:18,960
About damn time.

351
00:27:19,550 --> 00:27:21,400
Give me another one.

352
00:27:21,400 --> 00:27:25,390
Finally, they'll respect me.

353
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:04,750
And this man was watching her lecture?

354
00:29:04,750 --> 00:29:06,060
Yeah.

355
00:29:06,770 --> 00:29:11,860
The very guy I bumped into
on my way to the Professor's house.

356
00:29:11,860 --> 00:29:14,570
He might be the culprit.

357
00:29:14,570 --> 00:29:19,800
If he is, Kayoko Senba may be in danger.

358
00:29:36,310 --> 00:29:41,830
What ties my uncle and Kayoko Senba together?

359
00:29:41,830 --> 00:29:44,210
Maybe it is those bones.

360
00:29:46,980 --> 00:29:49,940
I got this piece of hair
from the Professor's place.

361
00:29:49,940 --> 00:29:51,210
Huh?

362
00:29:56,160 --> 00:29:57,960
P-Professor...

363
00:29:59,060 --> 00:30:00,900
Professor.

364
00:30:00,900 --> 00:30:02,750
Professor Ishimizaki!

365
00:30:07,480 --> 00:30:09,660
T-The police.

366
00:30:16,560 --> 00:30:20,700
<i>I only noticed the strand later.</i>

367
00:30:21,560 --> 00:30:27,150
If the DNA of this hair
and the saliva on this bottle match,

368
00:30:27,150 --> 00:30:31,860
then it means Kayoko Senba
visited Professor Ishimizaki.

369
00:30:31,860 --> 00:30:33,130
Right.

370
00:30:33,130 --> 00:30:34,910
You didn't tell the police?

371
00:30:34,910 --> 00:30:37,610
The police suspect me.

372
00:30:37,610 --> 00:30:40,690
They wouldn't believe that
I "happened" to pick up this strand of hair.

373
00:30:44,770 --> 00:30:46,140
Hirama, your phone.

374
00:30:46,140 --> 00:30:47,660
Huh? Fix that.

375
00:30:48,870 --> 00:30:51,550
Who the hell's calling now?

376
00:30:54,700 --> 00:30:58,190
Onodera. How's the article coming?

377
00:30:58,190 --> 00:31:01,100
I'm being hunted down right now.

378
00:31:01,100 --> 00:31:03,410
Huh? What the...

379
00:31:06,710 --> 00:31:08,120
Can you raise your voice?

380
00:31:08,120 --> 00:31:09,740
<i>Definitely not.</i>

381
00:31:09,740 --> 00:31:11,190
<i>I've been running.</i>

382
00:31:11,190 --> 00:31:12,730
From who?

383
00:31:12,730 --> 00:31:15,210
<i>Some man I don't know. He's tailing me.</i>

384
00:31:15,210 --> 00:31:17,480
Tailing you?

385
00:31:17,480 --> 00:31:19,850
What have you gotten yourself into?

386
00:31:19,850 --> 00:31:22,190
Were you investigating gang work?

387
00:31:22,190 --> 00:31:24,000
I was looking into Nichie.

388
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:26,000
<i>Nichie Pharmaceutical?</i>

389
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,080
<i>What, are they creating
a new drug or something?</i>

390
00:31:28,080 --> 00:31:31,330
The founder was involved in some deep shit.

391
00:31:31,330 --> 00:31:34,200
<i>They wouldn't hunt you down
for something like that.</i>

392
00:31:34,200 --> 00:31:36,440
<i>They're just ordinary corporate folk, right?</i>

393
00:31:36,440 --> 00:31:40,500
Then maybe it's Xin Ming Ah.
I met up with one of their men earlier.

394
00:31:40,500 --> 00:31:42,310
Xin Ming Ah?

395
00:31:42,310 --> 00:31:44,190
<i>A conglomerate from China.</i>

396
00:31:44,190 --> 00:31:47,160
<i>They're aiming to buy out
Nichie Pharmaceutical.</i>

397
00:31:47,160 --> 00:31:53,590
If they get me, I want you to disclose
all the documents I have at home.

398
00:31:53,590 --> 00:31:58,320
Write an article, post it online...
just do something.

399
00:31:58,320 --> 00:32:01,030
The keys to my apartment are in my mailbox.

400
00:32:01,030 --> 00:32:03,890
<i>Fine, just tell me where you are right now.</i>

401
00:32:03,890 --> 00:32:05,980
Right.

402
00:32:05,980 --> 00:32:07,950
<i>What? You alright?</i>

403
00:32:22,410 --> 00:32:25,930
I wanted to become a real journalist.

404
00:32:25,930 --> 00:32:29,830
But that didn't bring food to the table.

405
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:34,620
So I became like every other hoodlum.

406
00:32:37,910 --> 00:32:40,900
And now I'm paying the price.

407
00:32:40,900 --> 00:32:43,560
Just tell me where you are.

408
00:32:43,560 --> 00:32:45,060
<i>I don't know.</i>

409
00:32:45,060 --> 00:32:47,510
<i>I think I'm by the rooftops in Kanzai...</i>

410
00:32:50,080 --> 00:32:51,720
Onodera?

411
00:33:14,990 --> 00:33:18,470
Damn it, where the hell is he?

412
00:33:51,410 --> 00:33:53,890
- Go ask for details.
- Yes, sir.

413
00:33:55,350 --> 00:33:57,650
Tada.

414
00:33:57,650 --> 00:34:00,540
Hey, didn't realize you still did fieldwork.

415
00:34:00,540 --> 00:34:03,020
I'm looking for a reporter named Onodera.

416
00:34:03,020 --> 00:34:05,150
What happened here?

417
00:34:05,150 --> 00:34:06,900
Not another murder, is it?

418
00:34:06,900 --> 00:34:09,980
We're not sure yet.

419
00:34:09,980 --> 00:34:13,030
There's a corpse, but we don't know whose.

420
00:34:13,030 --> 00:34:17,210
It wouldn't happen to be this man, is it?

421
00:34:17,210 --> 00:34:18,740
I couldn't tell.

422
00:34:18,740 --> 00:34:20,060
Please, take a careful look.

423
00:34:20,060 --> 00:34:23,310
No amount of looking will help.

424
00:34:23,310 --> 00:34:25,160
It's just bones.

425
00:34:26,190 --> 00:34:27,620
What?

426
00:34:27,620 --> 00:34:32,450
The body's all bones. No decomposing, either.

427
00:34:52,310 --> 00:34:53,760
It was a match.

428
00:34:53,760 --> 00:34:56,200
[Complete DNA match]

429
00:34:58,560 --> 00:35:02,910
Then Kayoko Senba went to my uncle's house.

430
00:35:03,960 --> 00:35:06,040
She did.

431
00:35:07,500 --> 00:35:09,700
Ubongo!

432
00:35:12,270 --> 00:35:14,910
I lost again.

433
00:35:14,910 --> 00:35:17,680
Again, Grandma.

434
00:35:20,650 --> 00:35:22,430
Grandma?

435
00:35:23,230 --> 00:35:25,460
Again!

436
00:35:34,450 --> 00:35:38,310
Ask her directly?
Kayoko Senba would never entertain us.

437
00:35:38,310 --> 00:35:41,000
Then let's ambush her outside her place.

438
00:35:47,490 --> 00:35:50,050
It's time for your appointment at the clinic.

439
00:35:53,790 --> 00:35:56,700
Your father instructed me
to drive you there.

440
00:35:58,820 --> 00:36:01,160
Please, go on.

441
00:36:02,100 --> 00:36:04,430
I'll meet you at her place.

442
00:36:17,330 --> 00:36:19,860
You finally came to see me again.

443
00:36:19,860 --> 00:36:22,110
I was worried about you, you know?

444
00:36:22,110 --> 00:36:24,200
Sorry.

445
00:36:24,200 --> 00:36:25,660
Here.

446
00:36:42,770 --> 00:36:44,830
How do you feel?

447
00:36:45,620 --> 00:36:47,940
Fine, thank you.

448
00:36:47,940 --> 00:36:51,290
Your father was terribly worried about you.

449
00:36:53,120 --> 00:36:56,120
I'm sorry for causing all the trouble.

450
00:36:57,320 --> 00:37:03,190
Take it easy, I only want to talk today.

451
00:37:03,190 --> 00:37:04,580
Yes, Doctor.

452
00:37:07,110 --> 00:37:09,590
May I ask about your younger sister?

453
00:37:11,020 --> 00:37:12,370
Go ahead.

454
00:37:13,190 --> 00:37:16,440
Your sister suddenly came into your life,

455
00:37:16,440 --> 00:37:19,590
upon your mother's remarriage, yes?

456
00:37:20,130 --> 00:37:21,580
Right.

457
00:37:22,270 --> 00:37:24,400
How did it feel, getting new family?

458
00:37:24,400 --> 00:37:28,250
Were you happy? Annoyed?

459
00:37:30,900 --> 00:37:36,100
At first, I was too embarrassed
to speak to her.

460
00:37:36,100 --> 00:37:41,180
For an anti-social guy like myself,
it was quite the hurdle.

461
00:37:42,120 --> 00:37:46,950
Learning to talk to her took time.

462
00:37:48,480 --> 00:37:51,500
What's a particularly impactful memory
you have of her?

463
00:37:53,400 --> 00:37:59,920
I had once told her of a theatre I visited
on my way back from school,

464
00:38:00,650 --> 00:38:03,790
that only played the classics.

465
00:38:06,870 --> 00:38:12,540
Because my mother was a busy woman,
and I had lost my father,

466
00:38:13,160 --> 00:38:16,100
I would often kill time there.

467
00:38:16,100 --> 00:38:21,010
That theatre felt like my one oasis.

468
00:38:22,790 --> 00:38:27,170
<i>And when I told her this,
she seemed to have an idea.</i>

469
00:38:28,400 --> 00:38:29,980
Where are we going?

470
00:38:29,980 --> 00:38:31,600
To find my treasure.

471
00:38:31,600 --> 00:38:32,850
What treasure?

472
00:38:32,850 --> 00:38:35,370
Just follow me.

473
00:38:38,610 --> 00:38:41,280
<i>She never attended school,</i>

474
00:38:41,280 --> 00:38:44,650
<i>and spent most of her time at home.</i>

475
00:38:44,650 --> 00:38:47,750
<i>So in a way, she was quite sheltered.</i>

476
00:38:49,160 --> 00:38:50,860
We're here.

477
00:38:50,860 --> 00:38:52,900
- Here?
- Yup.

478
00:38:52,900 --> 00:38:56,530
Aren't we trespassing right now?

479
00:38:56,530 --> 00:38:59,910
My father's company owns the building.
Therefore, it's mine.

480
00:38:59,910 --> 00:39:02,410
That's some absurd reasoning.

481
00:39:02,410 --> 00:39:03,610
"We are vehicles

482
00:39:03,610 --> 00:39:07,770
programmed to preserve
the selfish molecules known as genes."

483
00:39:07,770 --> 00:39:10,050
Richard Dawkins.

484
00:39:11,130 --> 00:39:12,660
In here.

485
00:39:23,700 --> 00:39:25,220
Come on.

486
00:39:29,810 --> 00:39:32,250
<i>Did you pity her?</i>

487
00:39:33,370 --> 00:39:34,700
<i>Yes.</i>

488
00:39:36,150 --> 00:39:39,500
<i>I wanted her to have her fun.</i>

489
00:39:48,400 --> 00:39:51,270
- Scared?
- No.

490
00:39:51,270 --> 00:39:53,080
This room, here.

491
00:39:54,030 --> 00:39:55,460
You sure we're allowed in here?

492
00:39:55,460 --> 00:39:57,410
Of course.

493
00:39:59,790 --> 00:40:01,420
Found it.

494
00:40:03,400 --> 00:40:05,620
This is my treasure.

495
00:40:12,750 --> 00:40:14,510
We're going downhill.

496
00:40:14,510 --> 00:40:16,800
Watch out! Careful!

497
00:40:17,750 --> 00:40:19,840
- Over there?
- Yup.

498
00:40:19,840 --> 00:40:21,420
Alright.

499
00:40:22,110 --> 00:40:23,980
- Almost there.
- I'm getting tired.

500
00:40:23,980 --> 00:40:27,140
- Straight?
- That's right.

501
00:40:27,140 --> 00:40:29,790
Alright, keep pushing.

502
00:40:34,260 --> 00:40:36,010
You scared me!

503
00:40:37,020 --> 00:40:38,980
No, that's for the speaker.

504
00:40:38,980 --> 00:40:41,160
Wrong wire.

505
00:40:45,280 --> 00:40:46,950
Ready, and...

506
00:40:59,630 --> 00:41:01,960
- Done.
- Yeah.

507
00:41:03,410 --> 00:41:04,760
Haruka, sit.

508
00:41:04,760 --> 00:41:07,520
- I'll do it.
- No, let me.

509
00:41:07,520 --> 00:41:09,040
I want to.

510
00:41:11,370 --> 00:41:13,760
- Know how to use it?
- Yeah.

511
00:41:17,230 --> 00:41:19,180
- Ready?
- Sure.

512
00:41:21,380 --> 00:41:25,490
[Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer Presents]
[Random Harvest]

513
00:41:34,120 --> 00:41:39,180
<i>Our story takes you down this shadowed path,</i>

514
00:41:39,180 --> 00:41:43,370
<i>to a remote and guarded building
in the English Midlands:</i>

515
00:41:43,370 --> 00:41:46,230
<i>Melbridge County Asylum.</i>

516
00:41:46,230 --> 00:41:47,570
<i>Grimly proud to be...</i>

517
00:41:47,570 --> 00:41:49,870
Am I allowed to stay?

518
00:41:49,870 --> 00:41:52,050
Huh?

519
00:41:52,050 --> 00:41:55,000
You used to watch alone, didn't you?

520
00:41:55,690 --> 00:41:58,440
<i>to house the shattered minds...</i>

521
00:41:58,440 --> 00:42:00,790
- I want you to stay.
- <i>of the war that was to end war.</i>

522
00:42:04,360 --> 00:42:06,000
That's a relief.

523
00:42:11,040 --> 00:42:13,730
<i>Now, Mr. and Mrs. Lloyd.
I should warn you.</i>

524
00:42:13,730 --> 00:42:16,730
- How can I ever repay you?
- <i>That even if he is your son...</i>

525
00:42:16,730 --> 00:42:18,900
- <i>He may not recognize you.</i>
- For what?

526
00:42:18,900 --> 00:42:23,100
- <i>His memory is affected...</i>
- This.

527
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:25,310
<i>The result of shock.</i>

528
00:42:25,310 --> 00:42:28,500
<i>He was picked up by the Germans,
in a shell hole near Arras, in 1917.</i>

529
00:42:28,500 --> 00:42:31,100
Just watch the movie.

530
00:42:31,100 --> 00:42:35,780
<i>Close to death,
and with no means of identification.</i>

531
00:42:35,780 --> 00:42:38,670
<i>When he returned to consciousness
in a German hospital,</i>

532
00:42:38,670 --> 00:42:42,810
<i>he could remember nothing at all
of his past life.</i>

533
00:42:42,810 --> 00:42:48,040
<i>He had no name... He had no family
with whom he could correspond.</i>

534
00:42:48,040 --> 00:42:53,680
<i>Six months ago, he was exchanged
through Switzerland and sent here.</i>

535
00:42:53,680 --> 00:42:56,100
Such beautiful eyes.

536
00:42:57,140 --> 00:42:58,540
Huh?

537
00:43:01,440 --> 00:43:07,000
I want you to remember me
with those eyes of yours.

538
00:43:09,400 --> 00:43:11,090
Always.

539
00:43:15,750 --> 00:43:17,080
Sure.

540
00:43:42,360 --> 00:43:44,560
Don't move.

541
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:47,330
You must be admitted to a ward.

542
00:43:47,330 --> 00:43:48,850
What?

543
00:43:48,850 --> 00:43:50,850
Your father told me everything.

544
00:43:50,850 --> 00:43:53,770
<i>He said you experience intense delusions,</i>

545
00:43:53,770 --> 00:43:56,570
<i>and are a real danger to those around you.</i>

546
00:43:56,570 --> 00:43:59,370
You must be admitted at once.

547
00:44:00,240 --> 00:44:03,310
This was your plan all along.

548
00:44:07,850 --> 00:44:10,920
Stay calm, it's just a stabilizer.

549
00:44:37,510 --> 00:44:39,500
Wait!

550
00:44:45,410 --> 00:44:47,380
He escaped?

551
00:44:50,680 --> 00:44:54,050
Please don't cause any more of a fuss.

552
00:45:16,290 --> 00:45:18,120
Excuse me.

553
00:45:24,040 --> 00:45:25,160
Show me.

554
00:45:25,160 --> 00:45:26,470
Yes, ma'am.

555
00:45:39,480 --> 00:45:41,050
So this is...

556
00:45:41,050 --> 00:45:42,450
Yes.

557
00:45:47,410 --> 00:45:49,850
A skeleton from Roopkund.
